John Kelley
Political Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
The United States recognizes the work of UNSMIL and SRSG Tetteh’s political roadmap.
Our goal remains to see a single, unified government fully able to assert Libya’s sovereignty, take responsibility for its own security, and enable mutually beneficial commercial opportunities.
To advance this goal, we expect the UN to follow the strategic review recommendations and focus existing resources on UNSMIL’s core political mission, establish performance benchmarks, and increase efficiency in its operations.
While we voted today to adopt the resolution, I want to express the United States’ continued frustration with efforts to push distracting and divisive language, including on gender and sustainable development.
The United States rejects and denounces the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals and will no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course. The Council’s insistence to include these in today’s resolution is misguided and diverts focus from the political process.
Likewise, President Trump has made clear the United States will use clear and accurate language and policies that recognize women are biologically female and men are biologically male. The language in this resolution on gender does nothing to advance Libya’s security or prosperity.
The UN has become a forum for the propagation of these divisive ideologies that stir controversy rather than bring member states together to address issues of common concern. This is why we continue to stress the need for the UN to return to the purposes and principles of the UN Charter and focus on peace.
Thank you, Mr. President.
