Mr. Chairman, the United States requested a recorded vote on this resolution and is voting NO.
The United States is a world leader in promoting international cooperation to support innovation and scientific advancement for the peaceful uses of outer space.
For this reason, the United States supports the expansion of the Artemis Accords, grounded in the Outer Space Treaty. The Accords represent the best of multilateral leadership in civil space diplomacy and bring together a diverse set of nations with a shared vision of peaceful space cooperation. The United States welcomes nations who share this vision to join us.
The United States also supports UNCOPOUS as the body with the leading role on policy in the UN system on the peaceful uses of outer space.
However, we must vote NO on the resolution on “International Cooperation in the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space.” UNCOPOUS should focus on its intended mandate of promoting international cooperation. Unfortunately, the omnibus resolution dilutes the focus of UNCOPUOS by introducing divisive terms and concepts that are far outside UNCOPUOS’s purview. We regret that our suggestions to improve the text were not accepted.
For example, the repeated references to the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals impinge upon state sovereignty as a soft form of global governance. We no longer reaffirm them as a matter of course.
References to climate change distract from the core of UNCOPUOS’ mandate. In lieu of general references to climate change, UN resolutions should focus on specific environmental threats and sensible environmental protections.
Similarly, the references to gender are ambiguous and unclear. The United States expects that UN resolutions use terminology that clearly refers to women and men, girls and boys, and avoids ambiguous terms.
Finally, the resolution references the term “developing country.” We are concerned that the term has lost clarity and is being misappropriated by countries whose economic scale no longer justifies its use. We urge the use of more clearly defined terminology that reflects actual national economic conditions. Technical assistance should be based on objective criteria, not vague and inconsistent labels.
