Political Counselor
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you Madam SRSG for your informative briefing today on the situation in the Central African Republic.
We are encouraged by recent progress in the CAR, thanks in part, to MINUSCA.
The disarmament and demobilization of armed groups, including of UPC and 3R, has led to decreased armed group activity and improved security in the region of the country.
Progress in elections, including publication of the final voter list, marks an important step in the electoral process and the decentralization of state authority.
We are also encouraged by steps the CAR government has made in financing the National Development Plan, including at the Casablanca donors conference, although challenges remain.
We encourage the CAR government to continue pursuing reforms and diversified partnerships to ensure economic opportunities can contribute to peace and prosperity in the country.
Colleagues, despite progress, we remain concerned by persistent insecurity in parts of CAR, including spillover from the conflict in Sudan, persistent conflict over resource producing areas, and pockets of continuing ethnic and armed group violence and extortion.
Against this backdrop of progress amidst lingering challenges, we urge the CAR government to continue working with MINUSCA to consolidate security gains on the path to peace, prosperity, and self-reliance.
Consolidating security gains over the long-term will require government commitment to security sector reform and a professional and accountable national force that can safeguard CAR’s borders and protect its civilians.
As a Council, we should appreciate and support these peace and security gains, while ensuring expensive peacekeeping missions do not become permanently entrenched in the country.
With continued and future progress, including upcoming elections, we must always consider how MINUSCA can work with the CAR government to appropriately transition responsibilities to the government to ensure it is ultimately prepared to take on MINUSCA’s full responsibilities in the long-term.
In closing, Mr. President, we urge the government and the political opposition to engage in open and constructive dialogue to reduce political tensions in preparation for historic local elections.
We look to CAR’s independent electoral and constitutional institutions to faithfully uphold the law and fulfill their democratic duty to their compatriots.
Thank you, sir.
