AS DELIVERED
MATTHEW BOYLE: All right, Mr. Ambassador, really want to thank you for taking the time to sit down with us here. This is a really important room here. We see world leaders come through here quite a bit. President Trump spoke here just a few weeks ago. He talked a lot about how we need to reform the United Nations and make it – and questioned kind of the purpose of the United Nations. President Trump has really carved out in his second term here in the White House that he is the peace president, if you will. First off, talk to us about President Trump’s role as the peace president, and how we make the United Nations help him achieve those goals.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: Yeah, well, first and foremost, thanks for having me here in the General Assembly, in the United Nations. And people ask me all the time, what’s, you know, what’s my agenda as Ambassador here? And my answer is easy, it’s President Trump’s peace agenda, right? He is the president of peace. He has used the power of the presidency, the power of his personality, and the fact that all of these world leaders and the relationships he’s developed. They may not trust each other, in fact, oftentimes hate each other, but they trust him. And so, as he said right here in the General Assembly – when his teleprompter didn’t work. But I actually, I told him, I said, Mr. President, I think it was a great thing, because in many ways, it just shows you unscripted. Any other world leader would have melted right there, not him, because his convictions are so clear, his agendas are so clear, and he just kind of charged forward. What he really asked in that speech, and what I asked even in my testimony before the Senate, is, what is the purpose of the UN and how do we get it back to basics? It was founded in the wake of World War Two. It was really put together by those who won the second world war into one: how do we prevent a third world war? And then how do we bring peace and prosperity around the world and ensure that stays in place. So, like we’ve seen in our own federal government, we’ve got to cut out all the nonsense, all the climate and gender and woke agenda, and just as we’ve had to do that in Washington, we’ve got to do it right here in New York and get it back to basics, stopping wars, preventing wars, and keeping the peace. And that’s what the President’s all about, and that’s what I’ll be all about here as the Ambassador.
BOYLE: So, Mr. Ambassador, I think the President said that the potential for the United Nations during his address here, he talked about it as incredible. I think the word he used was tremendous, right? The potential is tremendous. How do we get to that point, though, where that potential is realized?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: So, look, I mean, I know there’s a lot of people out there, rightly so. I was a former member of Congress. If I went to a town hall in North Florida and talked to a bunch of mechanics, teachers, you know, firefighters. It’s their money – and we pay for, by the way, a quarter of everything the UN does the United States pays for. Is there money being well spent? I’d say right now, no, because it’s being spent on all of these other woke projects, rather than what it was originally intended to do, what President Trump wants it to do, and what I want it to do, which is focus on peace. And I would say to those who say, why don’t we just shut this thing down and walk away? Well, I think we need it to be reformed in line with its potential that President Trump sees. And I think my answer would be, we need one place in the world where everybody can talk. President Trump is a president of peace. He wants to keep us out of war. He wants to put diplomacy first. He wants to create deals. Well, there’s one place in the world, and that’s right here at the UN that the Chinese, the Russians, the Europeans, developing countries all over the world can come and do their best to hash things out. And I think if we, you know, frankly, give the UN some tough love, which we’re doing right now, and get them away from all of that other nonsense, whether it’s a climate agenda or the gender ideology or what have you, and get it focused back on creating peace deals and helping the President do what he does best, then we can help it realize its potential, and we have an entire strategy and plan to do that.
