Ambassador Dan Negrea
United States Mission to the U.N.
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. The United States thanks President Gatti Santana for her annual report on the efforts of the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals to bring perpetrators to justice for atrocities committed in Rwanda and the former Yugoslavia.
The success of the Mechanism and its predecessors—the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia and the International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda—has helped usher in a new era of accountability where perpetrators of atrocity crimes can no longer assume that they can escape justice.
While the Mechanism has wrapped up its active cases, we note the outstanding issue of Felicien Kabuga’s possible release due to his health, as well as the contempt proceedings that have followed the Ngirabatware case review process.
We appreciate the Mechanism’s efforts to carry out residual duties that are integral to the administration of justice—supervising the enforcement of sentences, monitoring cases in national systems, protecting witnesses, and
preserving and managing the archives – while also actively planning for the responsible transfer of appropriate functions.
In this vein, we applaud the continued work of the Office of the Prosecutor in responding to the high volume of requests for assistance from national jurisdictions, including with respect to requests for access to evidence, requests for legal, investigative, and direct case assistance, and requests for assistance with cross-cutting issues such as the challenges of fugitives and international cooperation. Despite the ongoing and active work in this area, we appreciate the Office of the Prosecutor’s review of its functions, including the transfer of its assistance functions to another UN office, as well as the transfer of other prosecutorial functions to national authorities.
The transfer of these functions is consistent with Security Council resolution 2740, which emphasized that the Mechanism was created to be small, temporary and efficient – its functions and size to diminish commensurate with its reduced functions. And in this vein, we look forward to the Secretary-General’s upcoming reports on possible archive locations for the ICTR and ICTY, as well as options for the transfer of functions regarding supervision of enforcement and pardon or commutation of sentences, as well as assistance to national jurisdictions on prosecution, in line with the Mechanism’s statute.
We also look forward to the next evaluation report by the Office of Internal Oversight Services in making recommendations for the further wind down of the Mechanism’s functions and note the successful closure of two of the OIOS’s four prior recommendations to the Mechanism.
Finally, we call on Member States to cooperate with the Mechanism to resolve key remaining issues, including to facilitate the closure of the United Nations Detention Unit in The Hague and the situation of acquitted and
released persons in Niger – both of which the Mechanism has noted would yield “substantial cost savings and advance the shared objective of a principled, fair and efficient conclusion to the Mechanism’s mandate.”
Thank you.
