Emily Kimball
Legal Adviser
United State Mission to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair.
The United States appreciates the Sixth Committee’s continued interest in this agenda item. We thank the Secretary-General for his reports, including his most recent report, which have usefully summarized the submissions made by States on this topic.
Over the years, the Committee has engaged in thoughtful discussions on universal jurisdiction, including on the concept’s definition, scope, and application. The view of the United States continues to be that basic questions remain about how jurisdiction should be exercised in relation to universal crimes, and that it is crucial to take into account States’ views and practices. These considerations are particularly salient given the importance of this issue and its long history as part of international law relating to piracy.
We remain interested in further exploring issues related to the practical application of universal jurisdiction. In that regard, we note the usefulness of submissions made by States to date, and the Secretary-General’s reports, in helping us to identify differences of opinion among States as well as points of consensus on this issue.
The United States continues to analyze the contributions of other States and organizations, and we look forward to reviewing further input from States and exploring these issues in as practical matter as possible, including through the working group that this Committee decided last year to convene at the eighty-first session.
Thank you, Chair.
