Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. Thank you, Deputy Special Envoy Najat Rochdi and Mr. Ramesh Rajasingham – forgive me if I got that wrong. But thank you for your briefings. And I would certainly like to thank Geir Pedersen for his nine years, now, of service as the UN Special Envoy for Syria.
Colleagues, when Secretary Rubio met with President al-Sharaa on the margins of the UN General Assembly’s high-level week just last month, he reiterated something that President Trump has said for months. Syria has before it a historic opportunity – a historic opportunity to build a stable, sovereign, and vibrant nation.
We continue to call on the Syrian government to seize this opportunity made possible by President Trump’s historic announcement on sanctions relief for the Syrian people. Further relief from sanctions is critical for the well-being of the Syrian people to give Syria a chance.
In that vein, we call on this Council to support efforts to ease UN sanctions on Syria, including the removal of sanctions on certain members of Syria’s leadership who are subject to sanctions under UN Resolution 1267, under that sanctions regime. And we thank member states for your constructive engagement on this effort that is certainly ongoing.
There are many challenges ahead for the Syrian government as it seeks to move on from five decades of the Assad regime’s oppression. The challenges as mentioned by the Deputy Envoy certainly include delivering on its promises of security, stability, and prosperity for all Syrians.
The Government should strengthen Syria’s national unity through the peaceful integration of all its constituents, including religious and ethnic minorities. The Government must show no tolerance for abuses, and all Syrians should have a meaningful stake in the country’s governance. There can be no progress without this assurance.
The United States continues to work with the Syrian government and Jordanian partners on a roadmap to reconciliation after July’s horrific violence in Suwayda. We expect all parties to approach the roadmap as an opportunity to work towards reconciliation and accountability to build a future together as a nation of equal rights and shared obligations.
We commend the Syrian government for its cooperation with the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons, as Syria and the OPCW work together to overcome challenges to accelerate the process of eliminating any remnants of Assad’s chemical weapons program.
Syria’s progress will also depend on the support of foreign partners. And for that reason, we welcome Syria’s efforts to strengthen ties with its neighbors. We will continue to help Syria rebuild those ties as a key element of regional peace and security. In that regard, we note Foreign Minister Shaibani’s positive meetings recently in Lebanon.
The United States has recently facilitated talks between Israel and Syria, with the aim of reaching a political understanding that would reduce tensions and stabilize the region.
We also welcome the efforts of countries in the region – including Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Türkiye, and Jordan – to support Syria’s institutions and economy.
And we urge the Government to take advantage of resources and expertise that are being offered to it from such organizations as the International, Impartial and Independent Mechanism and the Independent Institution on Missing Persons. These organizations were created to bring accountability and closure to the Syrian people, who have suffered so much, and have much expertise to share with the newly created national commissions working these issues.
And finally, we note the United States will continue working with Syria to prevent the resurgence of ISIS. It is critical Member States with nationals detained or displaced in northeast Syria relocate them as soon as possible and to support solutions for the remaining populations.
Thank you.
