Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
JESSE WATTERS: Mike Waltz is the U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations. All right, the tomahawks. What kind of damage could those do?
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: They can do damage, serious damage, but it’s to critical infrastructure the Russians need, particularly their energy infrastructure, but Jesse, I don’t think we’re going to need them. President Trump’s going to drive a deal. And the thing he does that is so unique in American history and around the world, he builds relationships. They may not trust each other, and they hate each other, want to kill each other, but they trust him. And when the NATO Secretary-General comes in and says, only President Trump could get those two on the phone and go from where – Biden was calling for regime change in nuclear-armed Russia. Gets them both on the phone, and they go from here, to here, and now to here, using leverage as only he could do. It’s – I think he’s going to get this deal done. But I’ll tell you, who needs a tomahawk when you have truths, right? When you can bend reality with these printers, right?
WATTERS: Also very powerful. So, this is leverage. The threat of delivering these tomahawks to the Ukrainians, which can do big damage inside Russia proper, and Putin trusts or believes in President Trump’s word that if he says he’ll do something, he will do something, and that you’re saying is going to bring Putin to the table?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: I think – it’s certainly compared to Joe Biden, right? And when he comes to see the President in Alaska, and he’s got B-2s buzzing overhead, they know President Trump means it. But there’s that, Jesse, but there’s also just how fast the President moves. I talked to a senior Biden administration official during the transition, and they were talking about scheduling foreign leader calls six weeks out, and they would develop the points and have the pre-meetings. He’s talked to six foreign leaders before he comes down to the Oval Office in the morning.
WATTERS: Yeah, it was all a waste of time.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: He’s driving the train. He’s not relying on the foreign policy elites or the establishment, and he’s surrounding himself with what? Business people who demand results, and move as quickly as he does, and moves out on his vision. And you get this to where, what you just saw Monday. I mean, four weeks ago, Israel was bombing Qatar, and four weeks later, you’ve got half of Europe scrambling to be on the stage with the President as he drives a historic peace deal. I mean, they were lining up like, you know –
WATTERS: He’s got the Europeans united. He’s now got the Arab world behind him. And what kind of move is he making in South America, and how could that affect what happens in Budapest?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, look, the President has been clear that we have to take care of our own hemisphere, that we have to secure America first, and that’s everything from the border to mass migration, the Panama Canal, the Arctic. We just created, actually, through the UN, a Gang Suppression Force to go after the gangs that are in Haiti to prevent migrants from crashing all over Florida shores. But he’s serious about these cartels and these gangs that are killing hundreds of thousands of Americans. We’re feeling that right here on our shore. And when America is secure, then everybody else is going to take notice.
WATTERS: Can we use the UN gang suppression unit in Chicago? They don’t want Trump’s help. Maybe they’ll let the UN do it. All right Mike, good to see.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Let’s keep the UN out of America. Keep them over in places like Gaza and Haiti. And by the way, we also just defeated a global carbon tax that they were this close to getting done. President, Marco, our Energy Secretary, Chris Wright, came in full force and knocked out something that everybody said we would just have to accept. And the Biden administration was shoulder shrugging. This would have been a global tax on everything that gets shipped anywhere and everywhere, and we just defeated that vote today. Thanks to the President and the truth tomahawks.
WATTERS: Well, my wife is thrilled. She’s driving back and forth to the mall all day, burning up a lot of gas. We love that. Thank you so much.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: All right, man.
