Explanation of Vote on UN General Assembly Fourth Committee Resolution on the International Day against Colonialism in All its Forms and Manifestations
Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternate Representative of the United States of America for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Mr. Chairman, the United States requested a recorded vote on this resolution and is voting NO for the following reasons.
First, the United States generally opposes the creation of new international days in the UN system. They add unnecessary costs, and work, to an already overloaded and costly UN agenda.
Costs and work that member states must bear.
This resolution, for example, proposes a new yearly General Assembly debate, including participation by the Secretary-General and the President of the General Assembly. Such efforts duplicate work already underway in the Fourth Committee and the General Assembly.
Furthermore, we question the drafter and co-sponsors’ intentions with this resolution.
Though this is disguised as a decolonization initiative, statements by Venezuela and Russia on October 3 indicate otherwise. They have made clear they are actually raising a number of unrelated accusations against the United States and other Member States under this new agenda item.
Second, we note the contradiction between this resolution and the actions of the drafter and certain co-sponsors. We question their putting forward resolutions espousing territorial integrity when they are taking actions inconsistent with that principle.
Both the drafter, Venezuela, and one of the co-sponsors, Russia, are taking actions that threaten or infringe the sovereignty of neighboring states. Venezuela has
sought to escalate its territorial dispute with Guyana. Russia, enabled by another co-sponsor, Belarus, continues to occupy Ukrainian territory and to conduct a devastating war in Ukraine.
These actions, when considered against the backdrop of this resolution, raise more questions about the intent behind this draft resolution.
Third, this resolution absurdly calls for the pursuit of reparations based on flawed historical guilt narratives.
These foolish suggestions pose a significant financial burden that taxpayers of member states would ultimately pay.
The United States categorically rejects this divisive concept and steadfastly opposes this resolution.
Thank you.
