Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Madam President, Mr. Secretary-General, colleagues.
The UN can be an important institution for solving international challenges, but it has strayed far from its original purpose. The UN has become bloated, unfocused, too often ineffective and sometimes even part of the problem.
Now is the time to embrace the opportunity and the urgency to create consequential and lasting reform. Now is the time for the UN to get back to basics, to renew its founding mission to maintain international peace and security, and to rebuild trust across the world that the UN can be a responsible steward of the precious resources that Member States entrust to it.
The proposed consolidation and reorganization of the peace and security pillar must strengthen conflict prevention and mediation, and bring greater coherence to work on drugs, crime, disarmament and counterterrorism. We invite further proposals to achieve these ends.
In the area of humanitarian assistance, we support efforts to deliver faster, leaner, more accountable support and to maximize impact on the ground, where it matters most, including through coordinated procurement and pooled logistics, global- and country-level common services, better data, and clearer division of labor to reduce duplication.
For the development system, the need for reform is urgent. The Secretary-General’s report acknowledges the “patchwork of entities with overlapping mandates, functions and fragmented governance.”
Simply put, the UN does not need the Department of Economic and Social Affairs, the Conference on Trade and Development, the UN Industrial Development Organization, Regional Economic Commissions, the Resident Coordinator System, single-issue geographic and thematic offices, numerous agencies, and UNDP all working on overlapping dimensions of development.
The same work can be achieved with greater impact by eliminating duplicative functions, consolidating redundant offices and programs – especially the proliferation of small, niche offices headed by Under- and Assistant Secretaries-General and of regional offices – and tailoring the Resident Coordinator system to the needs of countries, rather than a one-size-fits-all approach.
The Secretary-General’s report was noticeably weak on recommendations related to the human rights pillar despite significant duplication across entities and a proliferation of stand-alone, protection-related offices led by senior officials. We welcome further recommendations on structural reforms, including to reduce the number of mandate holders and Special Rapporteurs and cease the politicization of human rights across the UN system, including the persistent, unconscionable bias against Israel.
The time for endless talk about the need for reform, meetings about meetings, and unserious half-measures of reform, is over. The United Nations was created to prevent conflict and create space for nations to talk with each other. The United States will not shy away from the decisions needed to ensure this institution is leaner, more focused, and more effective in achieving its foundational mission of maintaining international peace and security while respecting the national sovereignty of Member States.
We look forward to working together to achieve these essential, durable, and consequential reforms.
Thank you.
