Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative to the UN Economic and Social Council
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam Chair,
While the United States respects the work of the Chair and this Committee, we want to be clear that we expect the Third Committee to focus on human rights and social matters strictly within its mandate. Too often, we see resolutions on regional and political issues that should be addressed in other fora. We ask members of this Committee to negotiate those arguments in the appropriate venue and not here in Third Committee.
This year, in the Third Committee, we will disengage from a majority of resolutions, precisely because we see the same contentious and irrelevant issues raised over and over again.
Third Committee resolutions should be short, actionable, and forward-looking. Instead, we consistently see resolutions with outdated references and restatements of documents not agreed to by all members. Too often, texts are repetitive, symbolic, or disconnected from realities on the ground. The United States will not continue to support work that strays from the Third Committee’s core mandate.
Rather than rubber-stamping every resolution that comes through this committee, the United States will focus our efforts on resolutions where American leadership can deliver real outcomes.
Additionally, many draft resolutions continue to include problematic language—calling for vague and even novel purported international rights and protections, along with social justice initiatives and mandates that interfere with Member State sovereignty and political independence. We will not endorse any language that undermines the inherent and unalienable human rights and fundamental freedoms of individuals in the United States.
Where we do engage, it’s because the issues are aligned with U.S. priorities: such as addressing human rights violations and abuses under oppressive regimes, combating trafficking in persons, and protecting religious freedom and the family.
The UN can be a useful platform when it reinforces freedom, peace, and human dignity. But multilateralism is not a virtue on its own. Success should not be measured by the number of resolutions passed, but by whether those resolutions actually help countries
protect against the abuse of human rights and fundamental freedoms, hold perpetrators accountable, and help the people of their societies live freer, more secure lives.
The UN can no longer afford to waste its resources where there is little or no impact. The number of reports, conferences, and negotiations has ramified beyond the point of ineffectiveness and waste, to a level of dysfunction that makes it impossible to carry out vital work. We must see less talk and more action at the United Nations.
The United States will continue to engage constructively behind the scenes—building coalitions, improving texts, and pushing back against harmful or distracting language. But when the final product is not in line with our principles, we will not hesitate to vote “no” or disengage.
Bottom line: This year, you’ll see the United States stand firmly behind only a handful of Third Committee resolutions. They’re the ones that reflect our values, advance our interests, and contribute to real progress—not just paperwork.
Madam Chair, colleagues, we look forward to working with you in the weeks ahead to make the Third Committee more efficient, relevant, and effective. Thank you.
