AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And let me thank, SRSG Onanga-Anyanga, Ambassador Idris, and ASG Pobee for your informative briefings, one and all. The United States appreciates your leadership and your cooperation.
Coordination and strong partnerships between the UN and the AU can help achieve peace, security, and prosperity in Africa.
With UN, AU, and regional peace operations active in some of the most fragile parts of Africa, we have a shared interest in aligning our doctrine, policies, and directives to make these efforts as effective as possible.
We share the Secretary-General’s recommendation to intensify efforts in order to ensure the partnership is mutually reinforcing, more responsive, and more effective for the people they serve.
The crisis in Sudan, continued conflict in Somalia, and fragile situations in the Sahel and eastern DRC underline this point.
On Sudan, we continue to call for an immediate end to the senseless violence. We welcome UN Envoy Lamamra’s effort to continue to coordinate the UN response with the AU and the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD).
On the DRC, it is important both the AU and UN continue to accompany the U.S. and Qatar-led peace processes to resolve the persistent conflict and bring prosperity to all.
The United States remains committed to the fight against terrorism in Somalia, but it is ultimately time for other partners with vested interests in Somalia’s security, stability, and future to increase their financial contributions.
Regarding peace and security in Africa more broadly, the United States views the promise of Security Council Resolution 2719 as a tool to promote more effective and sustainable African-led solutions to conflict.
It is an important innovation for strengthening the AU-UN partnership and leveraging the AU’s comparative advantages to addressing conflict in Africa. However, I must iterate we will not support any proposed implementations of 2719 that risks undermining the longevity of the tool, or that contradict the spirit and word of the framework.
Furthermore, the United States welcomes the AU’s Peace and Security Council’s formal adoption of the terms of reference for its Counterterrorism Sub-Committee, which will assist the AU to provide practical support to conflict-affected member states, strengthen resilience, enhance regional cooperation, and implement AU counterterrorism instruments.
In conclusion, Mr. President, the United States will continue to support close cooperation among the UN, regional organizations such as the AU, and sub-regional organizations to advance peace and security in Africa.
And I thank you, sir.
