Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And we also would like to welcome newly appointed Special Representative Jenča. We look forward to continuing our work with you in your new capacity as SRSG.
The United States of course supports peace and security in Colombia and justice for victims of narcoterrorism. In fact, history will look back on U.S. contributions to plan Colombia decades ago as a true success story for the people of Colombia. But unfortunately, the current Government of Colombia and continued politicization are undermining progress in achieving sustained peace in Colombia.
Colleagues, I would like to make three points.
First, the actions of the Government of Colombia continue to undermine chances for achieving a lasting peace.
President Petro’s policies on security and peace – both in Colombia and around the world – are frankly irresponsible. Failures to plan, to budget, to coordinate with its own government have led Colombia to greater instability and to violence.
Just last week during the UN General Assembly High Level Week, President Petro used outrageous rhetoric, right here on U.S. soil, to urge American soldiers to disobey lawful orders and to incite violence on U.S. soil. This rhetoric is an example of the broader reckless and incendiary actions of the Colombian government.
Secondly, the politicization of peace is a threat to sustained progress. Security and justice should be guaranteed to all Colombians, not be subordinated to political priorities.
The UN Verification Mission in Colombia was established to verify the demobilization of the FARC terrorist group. Unfortunately, over time, the mission’s mandate has broadened to reflect excessive political priorities, including transitional justice and supporting minority ethnic groups. The United States is closely examining this mission’s mandate and whether it merits continued UN Security Council support based on an assessment of the contribution it makes to peace and security in Colombia.
Thirdly, violence from armed groups continues to concern the United States. We too mourn the tragic assassination of Senator Miguel Uribe Turbay, an inspiring young political leader cut down while campaigning in the streets of Bogota. We agree, and we certainly urge, all actors, that political violence has no place in a civilized society, and we too urge the Government to do everything it can to protect the voices and lives of its political leaders.
In recent months, Colombia has been rocked by attacks by narco-terrorist groups on Colombian security forces and civilians. The violence and drug trafficking perpetrated by these armed groups, if left unchecked, can spread and jeopardize the safety of Colombians, the safety of everyone in the region, and certainly of Americans. We urge the Government of Colombia to prioritize addressing this threat.
And we are deeply concerned about the prospect of “Total Peace” negotiations facilitating impunity for these groups. The Colombian people, the people of the region, and certainly the American people, deserve justice and security.
Thank you, Mr. President.
