Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President.
Colleagues, the United States vetoed this resolution as it failed in a very fundamental task. It failed to condemn Hamas. And it failed to recognize Israel’s right to defend itself amongst other serious defects.
Council Members ignored when the United States made it clear that this resolution was unacceptable.
Hamas continues to this day right now as we speak to hold 48 hostages – those who remain alive are captive in a living hell. We have seen the chilling videos, we have seen the images of how Hamas is starving these hostages, how they are forcing them to dig their own graves on camera. And we have heard, just yesterday, I met with two survivors, and we have heard their heart-rending testimony. We have heard from freed hostages as well on how Hamas has brutalized and sexually assaulted them during their captivity.
The reality is that Hamas is responsible for starting and continuing this war. Israel has repeatedly accepted proposed terms that would end the war, including just this week on Monday, September 29, just 48 hours ago, with President Trump at the White House.
President Trump’s Comprehensive Plan to End the Gaza Conflict sets a path forward for the immediate end of the war, the immediate release of all 48 hostages, the complete disarmament of Hamas, the demilitarization of the Gaza Strip, and the economic redevelopment of Gaza.
This plan, which has been accepted by Israel and has broad support from a number of partners, from a number of Arab states, from Muslim majority states, from Türkiye, from Indonesia, will allow Gaza to begin a peaceful and prosperous future while ensuring that Hamas no longer threatens Israel, while ensuring that this can never happen again. We urge Hamas to accept the deal on the table. We call on all Member States to give this plan their full support and demand that Hamas immediately accept this agreement.
But turning back to the issue at hand, the resolution that the United States vetoed on September 18 had numerous serious defects beyond its failure to condemn Hamas.
Essentially, the United States made it clear that we will not accept a resolution that does not condemn Hamas, that does not call on Hamas to disarm and leave the Gaza Strip, that does not call for an immediate release of hostages, that does not acknowledge the problems with aide diversion in the Gaza Strip, and that makes any false equivalence between Israel’s right to defend itself and the actions of Hamas. We vetoed the resolution because it did not meet these conditions.
Colleagues, President Trump’s plan offers us the opportunity to free the hostages, bring an end to this horrific conflict and all its suffering, give the Gazans a future free from Hamas that they deserve, and pave the way for the entire Middle East to move to a new chapter of peace and prosperity. And we call upon all of you, all Member States, to join us in supporting it.
So my question to you all: Where is the outcry? Where are the demands for Hamas to accept this offer of peace? Where are the demands for Hamas to end the suffering? They could end it today by accepting this offer. And by doing that, we all, together, can ensure that this never ever happens again.
Madam President, I thank you.
